const faq = [
  { q: 'What is special about a raven\'s bill?', a: 'The common raven\'s bill is large, heavy, and arched — the culmen (upper bill ridge) curves downward toward the tip. This bill shape allows ravens to perform a wide range of tasks: tearing flesh from large carcasses; prying open containers; probing into soil; cracking open shellfish or eggs; manipulating and carrying objects; and communicating threat through bill-gaping displays. The bill\'s size and strength is unusual for a passerine and reflects the raven\'s dietary and ecological generalism — it functions as a multi-tool rather than a specialist instrument.' },
  { q: 'How large is a raven\'s brain?', a: 'Common ravens have one of the largest brain-to-body ratios of any bird species, comparable to that of great apes relative to body size. The avian brain structure differs from mammalian brain structure, but the pallium (the layer associated with higher cognition) is proportionally very large in corvids. Ravens\' cognitive abilities — planning, social intelligence, problem-solving, memory — correlate with this enlarged forebrain. In absolute terms, a raven\'s brain weighs approximately 15–20 g in adults.' },
  { q: 'What are raven wings adapted for?', a: 'Raven wings are long and broad with prominent "fingered" primary feathers at the tips — an adaptation for soaring and thermal gliding rather than fast flapping flight. This wing shape suits the raven\'s ecological habits: soaring over open terrain to locate carrion from height; riding thermals for energy-efficient movement; and performing aerial acrobatics (barrel rolls, inverted flight) in play and courtship. The wing shape is intermediate between the pointed wings of fast-flying species (swifts, falcons) and the rounded wings of forest birds — a generalist form for a generalist lifestyle.' },
];
